    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "de41db93_65c36852",
        "filename": "core/tests/benchmarks/src/android/net/NetworkStatsBenchmark.java",
        "patchSetId": 21
      },
      "lineNbr": 63,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-25T21:28:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "No I don\u0027t think there are any guidelines, nor do I know there could be any. Some stuff is clearly cheap, some stuff is clearly expensive, and a lot of stuff is somewhere in the middle and not obvious, and sometimes there is mildly expensive stuff with obviously desirable advantages and you have to take a non-obvious tradeoff.\n\nIt would be interesting to know how often this gets called, if apps can call this and with what implications (we used to have issues with GMSCore hammering the stats API every 20ms at some point), and how much more load this is in the great scheme of things (your patch obviously makes the code you\u0027re touching massively slower, but I don\u0027t have a good notion of how much that represents in stats processing generally, maybe it\u0027s negligible).\nUnfortunately I don\u0027t think the argument that most users don\u0027t have a VPN would work very well here because users who do typically use it most or all the time, and would then take the full brunt of this.\n\nMy general impression of this change though is that the multiplier in your code is mostly the number of interfaces and I don\u0027t think this is likely to grow to very high numbers. So we have processing that\u0027s in M*N but if N is always \u003c\u003d3 it\u0027s not the end of the world.",
      "parentUuid": "3ebe6b16_dcd6be7f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 63,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 63,
        "endChar": 30
      },
